From b767009fc1c4a828fc39c94be37951058678f4c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ebben Aries <exa@dscp.org>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:39:03 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] fix(clock): stop forcing a tooltip requery on every tick

Clock::update() runs every `interval` (1s in typical calendar setups)
and unconditionally called label_.trigger_tooltip_query() after
refreshing the custom tooltip widget's markup, regardless of whether
the pointer was even over the module.

m_tooltip_ is the same Gtk::Label instance embedded into the popup via
tooltip->set_custom() in query_tlp_cb(); mutating its markup in place
(the set_markup() call immediately above) already redraws it while the
popup is open, the same way any on-screen widget redraws when its
content changes. trigger_tooltip_query() isn't needed for that.

Under gtk-layer-shell, the tooltip is an xdg_popup anchored to the
bar's layer surface. Forcing a requery every second while hovering can
tear down and recreate that popup's role, requiring a fresh
configure/ack/commit round-trip with the compositor each time. If any
one of those round-trips lands out of order, the popup can end up
committed with a stale/empty buffer -- visible as the calendar
occasionally showing an empty box instead of its contents partway
through a hover, worse the longer/more often the cycle repeats over a
session.

Drop the forced requery from the periodic per-tick path. Content
updates continue to show live while the popup is open because the
underlying widget is mutated directly; a genuine new hover still
triggers query_tlp_cb() through GTK's normal tooltip machinery.

Calendar/timezone actions (doAction(), e.g. right-click to toggle
month/year mode) are a different case: they can change the tooltip's
content size, which a passive markup update won't renegotiate, and
they're discrete user-triggered events rather than a once-a-second
loop. So doAction() still forces an immediate update() + requery after
running an action, restoring the in-place resize/refresh those actions
had before, without reintroducing the periodic-tick churn.

That requery can't happen synchronously inside doAction() though: GTK's
own button-press handling hides the currently-shown tooltip via a path
that runs after the on-click signal handler returns, in the same event,
which clobbers an immediate trigger_tooltip_query() call made from
inside that handler. The previous once-a-second forced requery worked
around this by accident -- it ran from a completely separate timer
tick, well outside any click's event processing, so it never raced
GTK's own hide-on-click logic. Reproducing that outside-the-event
timing deliberately: defer the post-action requery via
Glib::signal_timeout(), so it runs on a later main-loop iteration
instead of synchronously in the click handler.
---
 src/modules/clock.cpp | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/modules/clock.cpp b/src/modules/clock.cpp
index a5e52e20..a3a69da7 100644
--- a/src/modules/clock.cpp
+++ b/src/modules/clock.cpp
@@ -285,7 +285,6 @@ auto waybar::modules::Clock::update() -> void {
     }
 
     m_tooltip_->set_markup(m_tlpText_);
-    label_.trigger_tooltip_query();
   }
 
   ALabel::update();
@@ -599,8 +598,26 @@ auto waybar::modules::Clock::doAction(const std::string& name) -> void {
     (this->*actionMap_[name])();
   } else if (auto key = name.substr(0, name.find(" ")); actionWithArgsMap_[key]) {
     (this->*actionWithArgsMap_[key])(name);
-  } else
+  } else {
     spdlog::error("Clock. Unsupported action \"{0}\"", name);
+    return;
+  }
+
+  // Calendar/timezone actions can change the tooltip's content size (e.g. month <-> year
+  // mode), which a passive markup update won't renegotiate. Unlike the once-a-second
+  // periodic refresh in update(), this is a discrete, user-triggered event, so forcing a
+  // requery here doesn't reintroduce the popup-churn hazard that was removed from update().
+  //
+  // The requery can't happen synchronously in this handler: GTK's own button-press
+  // handling hides the currently-shown tooltip via a path that runs after this signal
+  // handler returns, in the same event, which clobbers an immediate trigger_tooltip_query()
+  // call here. Deferring it to a follow-up main-loop iteration via signal_timeout puts it
+  // outside that event's processing so it isn't racing GTK's own hide.
+  if (tooltipEnabled()) {
+    update();
+    Glib::signal_timeout().connect_once(
+        sigc::track_obj([this] { label_.trigger_tooltip_query(); }, *this), 50);
+  }
 }
 
 // Module actions
